\section{Keep a Younger Store Private Until an Older Load Is Resolved}

Consider an illustrative in-order retirement policy with precise faults. A load reads ordinary memory; a younger store is to a test device. The device's accepting operation in this example is atomic and cannot fault. Address preparation can happen early, but device traffic must wait. The code is explanatory pseudocode, not VADL.

\begin{verbatim}
retire_head(q, device):
    head = q.first
    if not head.done: return WAIT
    if head.fault:
        discard(q)                     # younger stores are still private
        return take_trap(head.pc, head.cause)
    if head.is_device_store:
        if not device.accept(head.address, head.value):
            return WAIT                # a refusal has no effect
    else:
        publish_architectural_updates(head)
    q.pop()                            # acceptance and pop share a step
\end{verbatim}

The load at PC \texttt{0x100} waits for an access check while the store at \texttt{0x104} prepares a value of \(9\). Retirement first waits. When the load reports a fault, retirement emits a trap record for \texttt{0x100}; it does not write the load destination. The store entry is discarded, and the test device's log stays empty. The invariant is that no pending store changes the device log until it reaches the head and is accepted exactly once. The gate in \Cref{fig:visual-333} is the point at which that invariant is enforced.

